00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.800
as either TensorFlow or not.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.970
And so in this example, of
course, it would be TensorFlow.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.920
And to get this data
together, Sara mentioned,

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.560
we had the BigQuery
public datasets.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.520
And thanks to BigQuery's
very robust SQL engine,

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.490
we can do a couple of
pre-processing steps right out

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:50.840
of the gate.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.630
And there's a couple of really
useful functions on BigQuery.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:56.240
We really got a chance to dive
into some of the functionality

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.140
there that extends beyond
just the standard SQL

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:04.280
body of functionality and
operate on these big datasets.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:05.840
So one of the things
we first noticed

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.257
when we looked at
the questions is

00:06:07.257 --> 00:06:09.380
that, in this public
dataset, there's

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.780
HTML tags just everywhere.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:14.950
There's pre-tags, code tags,
paragraph tags, bolding,

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:16.730
italicized-- we don't
want any of that.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.010
None of that is useful for
identifying whether someone

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.510
is asking about a TensorFlow
question or a different machine

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:22.400
learning library.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.310
So we're going to
strip all those out.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:27.230
The tags also happen to be
separated with vertical bars.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.800
Not great if I want to make my
data look anything like a CSV,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.020
so replacing those with commas.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.400
And in this example, we're
going to pull out just related

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:38.990
machine learning libraries.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:41.300
We're not going to try
to tackle the bigger

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.120
problem of tagging questions--

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.460
even web development questions
as "TensorFlow or not."

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.222
And just stick within
this subdomain.

00:06:50.222 --> 00:06:51.680
SARA ROBINSON: So
hold on a second.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.760
Couldn't you just
write an IF statement?

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.240
I feel like most TensorFlow
questions have TensorFlow

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.850
in them at some point, so
you could do something like,

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.920
"if 'TensorFlow'" then
the tag equals TensorFlow.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.570
Maybe you don't even need
machine learning for this.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:03.240
YUFENG GUO: This is true.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.465
People do love writing
TensorFlow when they're asking

00:07:05.465 --> 00:07:06.590
about TensorFlow questions.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:09.390
And this applies to a lot of
questions on Stack Overflow,

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:09.890
right?

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.660
If you're asking a
question about pandas,

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:16.010
you're probably saying,
"in pandas, how do I do x?"

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:17.960
And then maybe you
paste in some code.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.630
And in that code, you write
"import pandas as pd."

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:21.860
Oh, there's pandas again.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.470
And so Stack Overflow
is an interesting space

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.080
where the tags oftentimes
line up directly

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.280
with the questions being asked.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.100
However, that's not
always the case.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.328
A lot of times for questions,
people will just dive right in.

00:07:35.328 --> 00:07:37.370
They'll just come out and
they'll just say stuff,

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:39.870
and they might not even bring
up what library they're using.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.930
They just want to know
this specific detail.

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:45.590
And they perhaps rely
on the tag to indicate

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.270
what library they're
talking about,

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.530
or what context they're using.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.800
And so we want to
train a model that can

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.410
be robust to these model names.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:56.293
Because if we just
have a model that

00:07:56.293 --> 00:07:58.460
just looks for TensorFlow
somewhere in the question,

00:07:58.460 --> 00:07:59.180
and it says, aha.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:00.417
There's TensorFlow.

00:08:00.417 --> 00:08:02.000
That's not a really
good model, right?

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.458
That's not really
machine learning.

00:08:03.458 --> 00:08:04.820
That's an IF statement.

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:07.160
And so what we can do
is take our dataset,

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.710
and we're going to replace
all instances of the word

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.170
"TensorFlow" in Matplotlib
and pandas and scikit-learn

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:16.740
and PD and PLT and TF and Keras.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.727
And we're going to replace
that all with the same word.

00:08:19.727 --> 00:08:22.310
And what better word to replace
our machine learning libraries

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.107
with than "avocado"?

00:08:25.107 --> 00:08:26.690
It'll prevent our
model from cheating,

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.580
and we'll have lots of
avocados everywhere.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.260
And everything is better
with more avocados.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.360
Your toast, your
datasets-- just everywhere.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:37.370
So lots of avocados.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:40.309
And so here's an example of
some of these pieces of data.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:42.601
We've already stripped out
those HTML tags and some

00:08:42.601 --> 00:08:44.059
of those new lines,
so everything's

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:45.600
compressed down now.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.870
And because a lot of times,
Stack Overflow questions

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:48.778
will be really long.

00:08:48.778 --> 00:08:50.570
You've got to scroll
and scroll and scroll.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.640
We want to make it so we can
see more than one question

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:53.150
in a window.

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:54.983
And you can see some
of these questions that

00:08:54.983 --> 00:08:57.410
are tagged with a couple
of different libraries.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:02.330
And you can learn about the
avocado dataset and import

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:06.260
avocado from avocado,
and avocado.layers,

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.420
and look at avocado
predictive models.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.020
It's avocados everywhere.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:16.450
So let's see what happens now as
we pull this data into AutoML.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:18.130
And we'll switch
over to the demo

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:24.950
and look at what it looks
like really on AutoML.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:25.470
Great.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:28.080
So we have here-- this
is the view of when

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.000
I pull the data into AutoML.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.710
That we can see some examples of
the various texts that we have.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:35.950
Lots of avocados everywhere.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.430
And we have our two tags--
"other" and "avocado."

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.220
There's just as much
avocado in the other tag

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.510
as there is in the
TensorFlow tag.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:46.682
So the model won't be able to
distinguish between questions

00:09:46.682 --> 00:09:49.140
that have lots of TensorFlow
in them and the questions that

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.316
don't.

00:09:50.316 --> 00:09:52.200
And so once we've
trained up a model,

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.780
it'll take us to the
evaluation folder.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.630
Training takes longer than
the time we have here,

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.120
unsurprisingly, given
the size of our dataset

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.880
and the complexity we're
hoping to achieve here.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.700
So AutoML Natural
Language uses a number

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.650
of different natural language

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.230
Processing approaches
to try to find the best

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:12.270
model that we can get.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.280
And so here--

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.470
I would say we did
pretty well here.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.330
All I had to do was hit "train,"
and then we just went off.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:19.360
We worked on our slides.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:19.950
We did other things.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:21.120
And at some point,
I got an email

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.070
that said, oh, your
model's done training.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:24.800
And so it was like, oh, great.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.470
Now I can just come
back and see how it did.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.040
And it really saved
us a lot of effort.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:31.260
There's no debugging of code.

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:34.320
There's no-- there's debugging
of the SQL code before,

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.820
but once we start training,
it got a lot easier.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:40.281
And so we can even check
out the confusion matrix.

00:10:40.281 --> 00:10:43.140
Because we only have those
two classes, we can show that.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.260
And what's neat about
this confusion matrix is,

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:50.580
instead of just having a
bland confusion matrix for us

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.950
to look at, we can
actually click into these.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:54.450
And we're getting
all this for free.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.000
I don't have to write any
code to go and identify

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.250
things like false
negatives and questions

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:01.890
that should have been
predicted Other in this case,

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.840
but were labeled as TensorFlow.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.960
Or questions that were predicted
as Other, but it should have--

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:09.150
or the other way around.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:10.660
It should have
predicted TensorFlow.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:14.760
So we can see some of
these questions that

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:18.782
are not accurate, and then
zero in on why that might be.

00:11:18.782 --> 00:11:19.740
We can adjust our data.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.240
We can adjust our approach.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.980
We can adjust how we
go about this process.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.640
And so it's really a
useful thing there.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.250
So let's go up, and we
can click Predict here.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:35.820
And what we'll do is grab an
actual Stack Overflow question.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.710
So let's say-- here's one.

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:41.046
It's about simplifying
URLs for .NET.

00:11:41.046 --> 00:11:46.583
And it's clearly not TensorFlow,
if I do say so myself.

00:11:46.583 --> 00:11:48.000
And what we can
do is we can paste

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.070
that text into the Predict box.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.950
So sometimes when you finish
training models, it's like, oh,

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.150
here's a REST endpoint.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:55.202
Good luck with that.

00:11:55.202 --> 00:11:56.910
Go figure out
authentication on your own.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.440
Write up some code, run
it, then you can call it.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.398
But it's really nice that
we have this text box

00:12:00.398 --> 00:12:03.960
here that we can actually
just click Predict and see

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.330
what we get back.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:10.170
And so you can test all sorts
of text into the body here.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.763
And so that's our
has_tensorflow model.

00:12:13.763 --> 00:12:15.180
So let's switch
back to the slides

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.510
and look at how we
can improve on this.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:24.090
Because knowing whether or not
a model is TensorFlow or not

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:28.520
is not that earth shattering,
if I do say so myself.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:30.600
You can do better
than that, right?

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.860
We can tag it across all
those tags that we saw--

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:35.018
Keras, Matplotlib,
pandas, scikit-learn.

00:12:35.018 --> 00:12:36.810
We should be able to
get a question and not

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.580
just say, oh, this
is not TensorFlow,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:41.220
but that this is pandas.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.688
And we can use that same dataset
we did before, but process it

00:12:44.688 --> 00:12:45.730
a little bit differently.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.397
Instead of saying
"TensorFlow or other,"

00:12:47.397 --> 00:12:50.010
we'll actually write
out the specific library

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.820
that it is using.

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.790
And our dataset across
these five tags--

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:59.970
there's a good number to
work with for each one, which

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:01.247
is nice.

00:13:01.247 --> 00:13:02.830
And so let's see
what that looks like.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:06.060
We'll switch back over
to the demonstration.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:10.110
And here, we'll jump straight
to the Evaluate screen.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:11.820
And we can see our five tags.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.860
Overall, the precision
and accuracy were good.

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:18.450
We can click into one of
these-- let's say pandas.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.930
And we can look at the specifics
around each of these tags.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.720
And again, see the true
positives, examples

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.270
of false negatives,
examples of false positives

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:29.250
across each of these tags.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:34.480
So you can click into these and
actually see specific examples.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.600
The full example,
not just the excerpt.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.248
And the predictions in
detail, as well as going back.

00:13:39.248 --> 00:13:41.040
And you can notice
there's a Delete button.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:42.600
So in some cases,
you might have data

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.267
that you're generating
yourself, and you

00:13:44.267 --> 00:13:47.400
might find, oh, that's just
an erroneous piece of data.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.862
I just need to get rid of it.

00:13:48.862 --> 00:13:50.820
It's actually not supposed
to be in my dataset.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.445
We're pulling data
from Stack Overflow,

00:13:52.445 --> 00:13:54.592
but your data might
come from user data.

00:13:54.592 --> 00:13:56.050
People might have
typed in garbage.

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.760
It might be malformed
in some way.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:01.230
So again, we'll go and
find another Stack Overflow

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:01.730
question.

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:03.550
Here's a question about Keras.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:06.760
And we can copy that question
into the Predict tag,

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.820
and we can test out how it does.

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:11.590
So we're hoping that
Keras will be one

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:12.890
of the top tags that come back.

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:15.345
So we see that it comes
back with a great amount

00:14:15.345 --> 00:14:15.970
of probability.

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:18.880
It also thinks that
pandas might be related,

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:21.670
and that, very
marginally, TensorFlow

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.960
also might be related.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.708
Another example
is with a question

00:14:26.708 --> 00:14:28.750
that touches across a
couple of different things.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:32.670
We've got pandas, we've got
DataFrame, and we've got Keras.

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:35.950
And so we can paste that
in and run Predict on it.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:38.890
And we get very strong
signals around Keras,

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:42.070
and there's a little bit
of TensorFlow on the side

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.670
too, because Keras is, after
all, now largely folded

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:47.320
into TensorFlow.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:50.200
And so one of things that might
be worth pointing out here

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:53.770
is that you'll notice the
numbers don't add up to one.

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:57.320
Because we're predicting
across five different classes,

00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:01.300
the breakdown isn't a strict
probability distribution,

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:03.760
because it can have multiple
tags that come back,

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:07.520
not just the one true
tag, so to speak.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.280
So let's switch
back to the slides,

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.730
and we'll click through the
screenshots in the event

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:14.590
of live demo failure.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:21.010
And now we'll move on
from the AutoML use cases

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.890
to a more custom machine
learning workflow.

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:25.280
And why might we
want to do that?

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.738
Why would we move
on from something

00:15:26.738 --> 00:15:27.730
that works really well?

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:31.450
We're seeing really
good results.

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:33.160
It seems that we can
just click Train,

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:34.665
and then we can
just get a sandwich

00:15:34.665 --> 00:15:36.040
and not have to
do anything else.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:39.970
But perhaps we want to
solve a more custom problem,

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.010
or maybe we have different
inputs-- not just

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:46.150
text but text plus images,
all in the same model.

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:47.860
Or maybe we just
want to learn more

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:50.650
about custom natural
language modeling

00:15:50.650 --> 00:15:53.770
and add that to our
machine learning tool belt.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:58.600
So we'll try to go through
this process in a way that

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.410
isn't just, draw a circle and
then boom, there's an owl.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.980
We'll try to get the steps in
between as well, so that we can

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.290
show that roadmap more clearly.

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:11.080
So I'll toss it
over to Sara, who

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:12.820
will go through this process.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:14.070
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks, Yufeng.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:15.695
So before we dive
into some code, let's

00:16:15.695 --> 00:16:18.080
talk about what we
mean by custom model.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:19.480
So when we say
custom model here,

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.040
we're going to be writing code.

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.400
In this example, we'll be
using TensorFlow and Keras.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:25.600
We'll write code to
pre-process our data,

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.270
define our model architecture,
define each of the layers

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:29.280
of our model--

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:31.390
we'll run training
and evaluation.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.120
We will deploy our model, and
then we will get predictions

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.402
from our trained model.

00:16:36.402 --> 00:16:38.360
So machine learning models
can, a lot of times,

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.230
seem like a black box
and a lot of magic.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.450
It's actually not magic.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:44.730
Under the hood, it's just a
bunch of matrix multiplication.

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:46.880
So if any of you remember
"y equals mx plus b"

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.138
from your algebra classes,
this may look familiar.

00:16:49.138 --> 00:16:50.930
So when you train a
machine learning model,

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:52.880
you have inputs, which
are your features.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.755
And then you have your
prediction-- the thing

00:16:54.755 --> 00:16:55.720
you're trying to label.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:59.000
And then you also have these
weight and bias matrices.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.820
And the idea is that as
you continue training,

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:05.300
you find the optimal values for
these weight and bias matrices

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:06.567
to get accurate predictions.

00:17:06.567 --> 00:17:08.150
So obviously, when
you start training,

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:09.567
these matrices
will be randomized.

00:17:09.567 --> 00:17:11.150
Your predictions
won't be very good.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:13.730
As you continue training,
they will get better.

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:14.800
YUFENG GUO: Hey, Sara.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:17.760
This all seems really reasonable
with numeric data here.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.060
We even took the time to
make sure these matrix

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:22.010
multipliers were correct.

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:23.990
How does this then apply
to other data formats

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:25.532
like we have here
with our text data?

00:17:25.532 --> 00:17:27.115
SARA ROBINSON: That's
a good question.

00:17:27.115 --> 00:17:29.665
May not be super obvious of
how to convert any type of data

00:17:29.665 --> 00:17:31.040
to a matrix, but
you can actually

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:34.620
represent pretty much any
type of data in matrix format.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:37.880
So in this image example, we
have this color image of a cat.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:40.920
And really, all an image
is just a bunch of pixels.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:42.830
So this particular
image, every pixel

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:45.290
has a red, green, and
blue-- an RGB value.

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:50.340
So this image becomes three
matrices of RGB values.

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.300
Let's say you have
categorical data.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.240
So let's say there's a
column in our dataset,

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:56.160
and it has three possible
values-- the string

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:59.498
sitting, stretching, or running
to describe different actions.

00:17:59.498 --> 00:18:02.040
The way you represent categories
for a machine learning model

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.380
is you do what's called
"one hot encoding."

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:06.930
So you create arrays where
the number of elements

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:08.610
equals the number of
possible categories

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:10.180
for that particular feature.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:11.730
And then all of the
indices are zero

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.980
except for one,
which corresponds

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:15.228
with that particular feature.

00:18:15.228 --> 00:18:16.770
Now, you may be
thinking, wouldn't it

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.200
be way easier if we just
encoded these numerically

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:20.880
as one, two, three?

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.710
That would be easier,
but then our model

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.850
would interpret these as
continuous numerical values.

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:28.942
So it would interpret stretching
as greater than sitting--

00:18:28.942 --> 00:18:30.400
running as greater
than stretching,

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:31.870
which isn't necessarily true.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:33.330
So we'd want our
model to treat all

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:37.090
of these different
categories equally.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:39.900
And then finally, for free form
text, which is the type of data

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:42.480
that we're dealing with,
there's lots of different ways

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.490
you can encode free
form text as matrices.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:46.530
We're going to be talking
about one specifically

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:47.730
called "Bag of Words."

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:49.350
This is my favorite
one to get started

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:52.150
with if you're new to
natural language processing.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.690
So what this method
does is it treats--

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:56.700
you can think of it as
a bag of Scrabble tiles,

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.325
where instead of a
letter on each tile,

00:18:58.325 --> 00:18:59.940
you have a word on each tile.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:01.560
So all of the inputs
to your model,

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:03.060
all of our Stack
Overflow questions,

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:05.030
are individual bags of words.

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:07.050
So it won't be able to
detect the order of words

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:09.930
in a sentence, but it will be
able to detect the presence

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.682
or absence of certain words.

00:19:11.682 --> 00:19:13.890
And Yufeng is going to tell
us a little bit about how

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:14.742
bag of words works.

00:19:14.742 --> 00:19:16.200
YUFENG GUO: So I
guess in our case,

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:17.730
though, instead
of a bag of words,

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:19.870
we might have some avocados
mixed into that bag.

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:19.982
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:19:19.982 --> 00:19:21.570
YUFENG GUO: A bag of avocados.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:24.620
So in this simplified
example, just

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:28.470
to talk us through the
concepts, let's pretend

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:31.600
that we do not know English.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.850
Our entire vocabulary--
we're learning English,

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:36.125
and the only words we
know in the entire world

00:19:36.125 --> 00:19:38.500
are those 10 listed there
under Vocabulary-- "dataframe,"

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:40.520
"layers," "series," "graph."

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:43.550
It's a very technical approach
to learning a language,

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:45.190
I suppose, to learn
these words first.

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:49.342
But be that as it may,
then we have these labels.

00:19:49.342 --> 00:19:50.800
We'll simplify down
and condense it

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.570
for this purpose to
just three labels--

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:54.970
"Keras," "pandas,"
and "Matplotlib."

00:19:54.970 --> 00:19:57.580
So we're going to try
to use Bag of Words

00:19:57.580 --> 00:20:01.610
to categorize sentences
as one of those labels.

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:04.180
But we only know
those 10 words, right?

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:07.630
So if we go to next slide, we
can see a example sentence.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:10.420
"How to plot
dataframe bar graph."

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:11.840
Not the best English, perhaps.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.678
But when we look at it, we
only recognize three words--

00:20:14.678 --> 00:20:16.220
"plot," "dataframe,"
and "bar graph."

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.460
The rest of it is all gibberish.

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:21.370
So pretend you don't know
what the words "how," "to,"

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:23.090
and "bar" mean at all.

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:25.400
That's just nonsense words.

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:27.460
And so when you look at
that, what you can do

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:30.940
is you can then encode, from
your very limited vocabulary

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:32.740
of 10 words, that sentence.

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:35.200
We can encode that sentence,
based on the position

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:36.360
within our vocabulary.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:38.690
So our vocabulary is
going to be ordered.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:39.850
And so we see, ah.

00:20:39.850 --> 00:20:42.430
In this sentence, there
is the word "dataframe."

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:44.247
It happens to be
the fourth word,

00:20:44.247 --> 00:20:45.830
but we're going to
say that it's going

00:20:45.830 --> 00:20:48.497
to turn on the first
bit in our array,

00:20:48.497 --> 00:20:50.830
because "dataframe" is the
first word in our vocabulary.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:53.680
It's the first word we
learned ever, let's say.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:56.452
And the fourth word in
our vocabulary is "graph."

00:20:56.452 --> 00:20:57.910
It's the last word
in the sentence,

00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:01.080
but we're going to turn on
the fourth bit in that array

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:02.500
to a one.

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:05.170
And so this is why it's
called a Bag of Words,

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:07.450
because we don't
care about the order

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:09.470
of the words in the inputs.

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:11.890
It's like we took the sentence,
broke it up into words,

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:15.490
and just tossed them into a bag,
pulling out one word at a time,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:17.200
and only recognizing
the ones we knew.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:18.790
So we pull out a word
and it says "dataframe."

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:19.110
Aha.

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:20.313
We'll turn on the first bit.

00:21:20.313 --> 00:21:20.980
Pull out a word.

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:21.710
It says "bar."

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:22.793
I don't know what that is.

00:21:22.793 --> 00:21:23.610
Throw it out.

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:24.540
Then we pull out another word.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:25.150
It says "plot."

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:25.210
Aha.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:26.303
I know what that word is.

00:21:26.303 --> 00:21:27.220
That's the sixth word.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:28.430
Turn on that bit.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:31.130
And so when you finish grabbing
all the words out of the bag,

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:33.850
you have an array that has
encoded the sentence based

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:37.510
on your understanding-- your
representation of the word.

00:21:37.510 --> 00:21:39.970
And you do that for every
sentence in your dataset.

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:42.100
And so now, each
example has been

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:46.180
encoded into this array that
has ones for all the words

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:49.660
that showed up in each of
those respective sentences.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:53.110
And so this is a graphical
representation of that.

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:55.660
Each word goes to the different
spots in our vocabulary

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:57.400
and yields the ones
and zeros, and you

00:21:57.400 --> 00:21:59.140
can do the same thing
for the prediction.

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:00.200
It's a little more
straightforward.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:02.140
There's only three
words, and so whichever

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:05.670
ones are the correct labels
for a given sentence,

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:08.530
we'll turn on with ones
and leave the other values

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:11.030
as zeros.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:13.360
And so this brings us
back to our matrices.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:14.152
SARA ROBINSON: Yes.

00:22:14.152 --> 00:22:16.780
So now we know how to encode
our text as Bag of Words.

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:18.670
So this is what the inputs
will look like to our model.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:21.045
For this simple example, we
just have a vocabulary of 10.

00:22:21.045 --> 00:22:23.500
As you'll see in a moment,
we'll use a larger vocabulary

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:24.530
for our Stack Overflow dataset.

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:25.850
YUFENG GUO: It's a
little bit larger.

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:26.320
SARA ROBINSON: A little bigger.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:26.880
YUFENG GUO: Yeah.

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.463
SARA ROBINSON: And
then our prediction

00:22:28.463 --> 00:22:31.000
is just an array with the
size of the number of labels

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.580
that we're using.

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.263
So how are we going to
build our custom model?

00:22:36.263 --> 00:22:38.430
We're going to be using a
variety of different tools

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:40.122
to pre-process our data.

00:22:40.122 --> 00:22:41.580
We'll use BigQuery
to get the data.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:42.660
As Yufeng already
showed us, we're

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:44.340
going to use the
exact same dataset

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:47.740
to train our custom model as
he used for the AutoML version.

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:49.740
We'll use pandas, which
is a really handy Python

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:51.640
library for
examining our dataset

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:54.160
and doing some feature
transformations.

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:56.850
We'll use a utility
function from scikit-learn

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.820
to encode our labels
as multi-hot arrays.

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:02.670
And then finally, we'll use
Keras to convert our text

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:05.797
to Bag of Word matrices.

00:23:05.797 --> 00:23:07.880
We're going to use TensorFlow
to build our models.

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:09.910
Specifically, the tf.keras API.

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:15.090
We will run training
and evaluation in Colab.

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:18.850
Colab is a cloud-hosted
browser based Python notebook.

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:21.050
And since our model
trains pretty fast,

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:22.660
we can run it right in Colab.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:25.630
Then we will deploy it
on Cloud AI Platform

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:28.606
and generate some predictions.

00:23:28.606 --> 00:23:32.080
So what will the code look
like to encode our text

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:33.640
as a Bag of Words?

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:35.977
Luckily, we don't
have to do the process

00:23:35.977 --> 00:23:37.810
of getting the top words
from our vocabulary

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.070
and converting them
to matrices by hand.

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:42.520
Keras has a utility function
to help us with this.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:43.870
It's called a tokenizer.

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:47.290
So we instantiate our tokenizer
by telling it how many words we

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:48.760
want it to collect.

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:51.890
So it'll take the top 400
words from our vocabulary

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:52.730
in this example.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:54.370
Now, this is a hyperparameter.

00:23:54.370 --> 00:23:55.510
You should try with
different values,

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:57.052
depending on the
size of your dataset

00:23:57.052 --> 00:24:01.750
and the total number of unique
words in your text corpus.

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:04.750
So then we train the
tokenizer on our text data

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:08.530
by calling fit_on_text, passing
in our train_questions strings,

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:11.200
and then we convert
our text questions

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:14.980
to Bag of WORDS matrices using
the texts_to_matrix function.

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:20.550
And then we will build
our model architecture.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:23.960
So we're going to do this using
the Keras sequential model API.

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:25.918
This is my favorite API
for building models.

00:24:25.918 --> 00:24:27.710
Essentially, it lets
you define your models

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:29.040
as a stack of layers.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:31.220
So it'll be really easy
in code to visualize

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:32.400
what our model looks like.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.400
This is going to be a
deep neural network, which

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:36.440
means that it will
have some hidden layers

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:39.902
in between our input
and our output layer.

00:24:39.902 --> 00:24:41.360
So our first layer
is going to take

00:24:41.360 --> 00:24:44.205
an input of our vocab
size, which is 400.

00:24:44.205 --> 00:24:47.150
So it's going to take our
400-element Bag of Words array,

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:48.770
and then it's going
to convert that

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:51.395
into a layer of 50 neurons.

00:24:51.395 --> 00:24:53.270
And the way it's going
to do that computation

00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:56.427
is through an activation
function, which is called relu.

00:24:56.427 --> 00:24:58.010
The great part about
Keras is we don't

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.320
have to worry how that's
implemented under the hood.

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:04.120
We just need to know the right
activation function to use.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:05.870
We're going to have
one more hidden layer.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:09.010
So our model is going to
take that 50-neuron layer

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:11.320
and transform it into
a 25-neuron layer.

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:14.140
Again, the size of your layers
is another hyperparameter

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:16.700
that you can use to experiment
with different values.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:18.400
So the outputs of
these hidden layers

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:20.192
aren't going to really
be meaningful to us.

00:25:20.192 --> 00:25:23.260
Our model is just using them to
represent complex relationships

00:25:23.260 --> 00:25:24.080
in our data.

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:27.500
But what we do care about
is our output layer.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:29.560
So our output layer is
going to be five neurons,

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:32.920
because there is five
possible tags in our dataset.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:36.218
And we're going to use the
sigmoid activation function.

00:25:36.218 --> 00:25:38.260
YUFENG GUO: So Sara, let's
talk a little bit more

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:40.120
about why we're
using sigmoid here.

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:42.400
A lot of times, we see
different activation functions

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:43.510
in neural networks.

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.430
And oftentimes in
particular, softmax

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:50.290
is a common other
alternative activation

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:51.407
function that's chosen.

00:25:51.407 --> 00:25:52.990
SARA ROBINSON: That's
a good question.

00:25:52.990 --> 00:25:54.365
So remember, for
this model, it's

00:25:54.365 --> 00:25:55.870
a multi-label,
multi-class model.

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:58.930
So that means that our model
could predict multiple tags

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:00.790
for a given question.

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:03.850
So what sigmoid will do is
it'll take each of those five

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:06.880
elements in our output array,
which correspond to the labels,

00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:09.350
and it'll return a
value from zero to one,

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.160
indicating the probability
that that question corresponds

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:13.360
with that label.

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:15.490
And the values will
not add up to one,

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:18.100
since there could be more
than one label per question.

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:20.690
You said you've seen softmax
a lot, which is a good point.

00:26:20.690 --> 00:26:22.870
If you instead had a
model where you could only

00:26:22.870 --> 00:26:25.810
have exactly one
possible label per input,

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:28.300
you would use softmax as
your activation function.

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:29.967
And what that would
do is it would still

00:26:29.967 --> 00:26:33.760
return that five-element
array, but all of the inputs

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.450
would add up to one.

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:38.110
So here's an example
of our machine learning

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:39.410
model's output using sigmoid.

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:40.910
And we can see that
in this example,

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:43.450
there is a high probability
that this particular question

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:47.590
corresponds with the first
and last label in our dataset.

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:49.780
Finally, we'll compile
our model so that we can

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:51.670
run training and evaluation.

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:53.800
We pass it the loss
function we want to use.

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:56.622
This is how our model calculates
the error between what

00:26:56.622 --> 00:26:58.580
it predicted and what it
should have predicted.

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:01.690
And this is the thing that our
model is trying to minimize.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:03.700
The optimizer we
choose tells our model

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:06.430
how it will optimize
those weight and bias

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:10.290
matrices after each iteration.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:12.140
So running training
and evaluation in Keras

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:13.223
is pretty straightforward.

00:27:13.223 --> 00:27:16.520
We call model.fit, we pass it
our train questions encoded

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:19.565
as Bag of Words,
our train labels--

00:27:19.565 --> 00:27:21.440
we're going to run
training for three epochs.

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:25.070
This means that our model
iterate over our entire dataset

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:26.600
three times.

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:29.490
The batch size is 64, which
means that our model will

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.430
examine 64 elements
from our dataset

00:27:31.430 --> 00:27:34.580
at once before it
updates the weights.

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:37.040
And then to evaluate our
model on our test data on data

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:40.370
that it's never seen before,
we just call model.evaluate.

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:43.310
We pass it our test questions,
our test tags, and the batch

00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:46.270
size.

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.450
Finally, we're going
to deploy our model

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.940
on AI Platform with this
new alpha feature called

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:52.110
Custom Code.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:55.590
Custom Code lets you run custom
server side Python code as part

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:57.400
of your prediction request.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.640
So this lets you configure
how you pass your inputs

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:01.770
to your model, how you
format them, and also

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:03.680
the format of the
prediction output.

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:05.430
So we're going to make
use of this feature

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:07.277
so that our client
can be super simple.

00:28:07.277 --> 00:28:09.360
All we want our client to
do is just pass the text

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:10.360
to our model.

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:12.360
And then on the server,
we'll use that tokenizer

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:14.820
we created to convert
it to a Bag of Words

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:17.680
before we generate
the prediction.

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:19.360
If you want to try
custom code, stay

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:21.130
