and we will call our custom
model prediction class

00:32:05.830 --> 00:32:07.427
and generate a prediction.

00:32:07.427 --> 00:32:09.010
So we can see that
our model predicted

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:12.370
Keras for the first one
with 91% probability,

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:16.330
and then pandas for the second
one, it looks like, with 89%.

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:17.470
So our model's working.

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:21.920
It's still just local
to this Colab instance.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:26.340
So the next step is to deploy
it to Cloud AI Platform.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:28.280
So what we're
going to do here is

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:31.130
we will package up those
scripts that I just created.

00:32:31.130 --> 00:32:34.050
I saved them to my
local Colab instance.

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:38.510
And then we're going to copy our
saved model and our tokenizer

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:40.550
to a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:32:40.550 --> 00:32:43.310
So I'll run this command.

00:32:43.310 --> 00:32:47.570
And then the next command
will package up all of my code

00:32:47.570 --> 00:32:50.480
and set my current
projects to the one

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:51.650
where I'm hosting this demo.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:00.620
And now, we're going to
run the deploy command.

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:06.050
I'm going to change the name
of the model to next2019,

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:08.910
and we'll run that
deploy command.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:11.200
And if we go over
here to our model--

00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:12.770
let me zoom in--

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:17.110
and refresh, we should see
that new version deploying.

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:19.480
So while that deploys,
let's go back to the slides.

00:33:24.730 --> 00:33:27.520
And I'm going to talk
about one more thing.

00:33:27.520 --> 00:33:29.520
So it's really important
to remember that behind

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:31.260
these machines are humans.

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:34.140
It may sound really
obvious, but as people

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:36.240
that are involved in
any part of the model

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:38.280
building or
deploying process, we

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:40.740
are responsible for the
predictions that are generated

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:42.222
by our machine learning models.

00:33:42.222 --> 00:33:43.680
So this means it's
really important

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:45.960
to take steps to make sure
that your training data is

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:48.360
representative of
the people that are

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:49.830
going to be using your product.

00:33:49.830 --> 00:33:52.740
So one example is, let's say
we train a model to identify

00:33:52.740 --> 00:33:54.420
10 different types
of cat breeds,

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:56.250
and we train it on
thousands of images.

00:33:56.250 --> 00:33:58.080
The accuracy is really good--

00:33:58.080 --> 00:33:59.610
99%.

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:01.710
We deploy it, and
someone comes along

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:03.360
and uploads a dog picture.

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:05.700
Now, our model's
entire world view

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:07.650
is just those 10
cat breeds, so it's

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:09.690
going to probably try
to classify the dog

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:11.820
photo into one of
those cat breeds,

00:34:11.820 --> 00:34:13.548
maybe even with high confidence.

00:34:13.548 --> 00:34:15.090
So it's really
important to make sure

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:17.159
that your training
data is representative

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:18.600
of the people using
your products,

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:21.659
and also that your model
is using the right features

00:34:21.659 --> 00:34:23.489
to make those predictions.

00:34:23.489 --> 00:34:26.370
The good news is, there's
lots of great open frameworks

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:27.719
to help with this.

00:34:27.719 --> 00:34:29.980
I'm going to use one in
particular called SHAP.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.230
It's an open source framework.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.179
You can find it on GitHub.

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:35.909
And what SHAP does is
it lets us interpret

00:34:35.909 --> 00:34:38.580
the output of any
machine learning model,

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:40.050
and it can tell
us which features

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:44.920
are most impactful in making
our models' predictions.

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:46.270
The way that we use SHAP--

00:34:46.270 --> 00:34:48.520
first we import it, and
then we create what's

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:50.030
called an explainer object.

00:34:50.030 --> 00:34:52.030
There's a bunch of different
types of explainers

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:53.207
as provided by SHAP.

00:34:53.207 --> 00:34:54.790
We're going to use
the deep explainer,

00:34:54.790 --> 00:34:56.380
since we have a deep model.

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:58.960
And we pass our deep
explainer our model,

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:01.380
and then a subset of
our training data.

00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:04.780
And then we can call SHAP
values on our explainer objects

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:06.310
to get the attribution values.

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:08.182
And what these
attribution values are is

00:35:08.182 --> 00:35:10.390
it's going to be an array
of the length of the number

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:14.260
of features we have, with
attribution values on how

00:35:14.260 --> 00:35:17.090
each feature impacted
our prediction.

00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:18.940
So high positive
values would indicate

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:21.460
that that particular feature
pushed our model's prediction

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:23.980
up, and low negative
values indicate that it

00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:25.930
pushed our prediction down.

00:35:25.930 --> 00:35:27.580
YUFENG GUO: So Sara,
this really sounds

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.730
straightforward for your
standard structured dataset.

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:32.770
You've got a CSV file,
you've got columns

00:35:32.770 --> 00:35:35.890
with all sorts of
different attributes--

00:35:35.890 --> 00:35:37.420
but we've got text data here.

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:39.310
It's just free form
all over the place.

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:40.780
Avocados everywhere.

00:35:40.780 --> 00:35:43.437
How can we capture feature
importance in a text model?

00:35:43.437 --> 00:35:45.020
SARA ROBINSON: That's
a good question.

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:48.010
So since we're using
a Bag of Words model

00:35:48.010 --> 00:35:50.830
and we've got a 400-word
vocabulary, essentially

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:53.860
what SHAP will do is it'll
treat each of those 400 words

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:54.890
as different features.

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:57.250
So we can look at the
attribution value for each word

00:35:57.250 --> 00:35:59.230
to determine which
words signaled

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:00.592
our model's predictions.

00:36:00.592 --> 00:36:02.800
YUFENG GUO: So it's like
having a really wide table--

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:03.670
400 columns?

00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:04.015
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:36:04.015 --> 00:36:04.360
YUFENG GUO: Gotcha.

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:05.693
SARA ROBINSON: 400-column table.

00:36:05.693 --> 00:36:06.460
YUFENG GUO: Great.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:08.877
SARA ROBINSON: So what I've
done is create a visualization

00:36:08.877 --> 00:36:12.040
to highlight the top five
signaling words in blue,

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:13.990
and the lowest
signaling words in red.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.030
So what we have
here is a question

00:36:16.030 --> 00:36:18.370
that is about
Matplotlib, and our model

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:21.370
is picking up on these specific
words to make the prediction.

00:36:21.370 --> 00:36:25.960
So it's picking up on the words
"color," "plt," "fig," "ax."

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:29.740
And then words like
"data" or the number four

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:32.710
are contributing least to
our model's predictions.

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:34.210
So this is good,
because it shows us

00:36:34.210 --> 00:36:36.580
that our model is
working relatively well.

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:38.170
If instead, it was
picking up on words

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.850
like "for" or "in" or
"the" to make predictions,

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:45.250
we may want to go back and
revisit our training data.

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:47.440
Here's an example
for TensorFlow.

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:50.230
And I actually used SHAP
to help improve this demo.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:52.900
So the first time that we ran
it, I sent Yufeng a screenshot.

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:53.817
I was like, hey, look.

00:36:53.817 --> 00:36:55.710
It's using the word
"TensorFlow" to predict

00:36:55.710 --> 00:36:56.713
that this is TensorFlow.

00:36:56.713 --> 00:36:58.630
Maybe we should take
that out of the question.

00:36:58.630 --> 00:36:59.370
YUFENG GUO: Perhaps, yeah.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:01.410
SARA ROBINSON: So that's how
we decided to use the avocado

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:03.870
replacement, to make sure that
our model was working really

00:37:03.870 --> 00:37:04.872
well.

00:37:04.872 --> 00:37:06.330
So for the TensorFlow
example, it's

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:10.950
picking up on words like
"variable," "session," "graph."

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:12.720
This is a pandas example.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:14.850
It picks up on
"column," "dataframe,"

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:18.550
and the negative signal
words are "for" and "each."

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:22.130
And so let's see
this SHAP in action.

00:37:22.130 --> 00:37:24.920
So if we could go
back to the demo,

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:28.480
it looks like our next2019
model finished deploying, so--

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:29.440
YUFENG GUO: Hooray.

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:30.440
SARA ROBINSON: Yes.

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:32.910
So we can go generate
some predictions.

00:37:32.910 --> 00:37:35.870
So we're going to write these
predictions to a text file,

00:37:35.870 --> 00:37:39.358
and I'm going to change
the version that I'm

00:37:39.358 --> 00:37:40.400
getting predictions from.

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:43.340
And I'm using G Cloud
here to get predictions

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:44.340
on my trained model.

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:45.860
And we can see that it works.

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:48.110
We get two arrays
of five elements,

00:37:48.110 --> 00:37:51.080
and we will visualize those
predictions a little bit nicer

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:52.190
here.

00:37:52.190 --> 00:37:55.160
So we can see that for the
first one, it predicted Keras.

00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:57.920
The second one, it
predicted pandas.

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:00.710
So now comes interpreting
our model with SHAP.

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:05.090
So to do that, we will first pip
install SHAP and this library

00:38:05.090 --> 00:38:07.880
called Colored, which I
use to color the output

00:38:07.880 --> 00:38:10.040
text in blue and red.

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:14.460
So since those are not included
in Colab, we will install them.

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:16.550
And once they're
done installing,

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:19.100
we will create our
SHAP explainer, just

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:21.470
like the code that I
showed on the slide.

00:38:21.470 --> 00:38:23.420
And our Keras
tokenizer is actually

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:26.650
a dictionary corresponding
with word frequencies.

00:38:26.650 --> 00:38:29.150
We are going to turn
this into a list

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:32.660
so that we can use it to look
up the corresponding words

00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:35.860
with each feature
attribution number.

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:39.460
So one handy function that
SHAP has is this summary plot

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:40.610
function.

00:38:40.610 --> 00:38:42.520
And what this tells
us is the words

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:45.160
that have the highest magnitude
in our model's prediction,

00:38:45.160 --> 00:38:47.562
so it's not necessarily
the highest positive value.

00:38:47.562 --> 00:38:49.270
It could be high
negative values as well.

00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:51.400
So we can see, for
example, the effects

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:53.740
that each word has on a
particular prediction.

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:56.890
So "dataframe" is impacting
panda's predictions

00:38:56.890 --> 00:38:58.550
most, which make sense.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:00.430
It's probably a
negative signal word

00:39:00.430 --> 00:39:03.040
for TensorFlow and Matplotlib.

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:05.830
And then for "plot," we
can see that's signaling

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:08.800
Matplotlib most, and
perhaps a negative signal

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:10.090
word for pandas.

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:14.710
So you can see a couple examples
of the top signal words there.

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:18.250
And I've written this
function to color our text--

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:20.890
the top five highest and
lowest signaling words.

00:39:20.890 --> 00:39:24.180
So we'll just print a
couple of examples here.

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:26.280
So this is a pandas example.

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:30.230
We can see it uses the words
"column," not a number.

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:31.940
Generic words like
"use" or "does"

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:34.520
don't signal the
predictions too much.

00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:37.010
For TensorFlow, we
got "sess," "ops,"

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:40.070
"tensor," and these
words that are related.

00:39:40.070 --> 00:39:42.320
Some of them to other
labels in our dataset

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:44.570
are pushing the
prediction down, so those

00:39:44.570 --> 00:39:45.920
are negative signal words.

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:47.480
And then I'll just scroll
through some other examples

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:47.980
here.

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:49.760
We have another Keras example.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:53.535
Words like "lstm,"
"layer," "dense,"

00:39:53.535 --> 00:39:54.910
which are pretty
common words you

00:39:54.910 --> 00:39:57.820
may recognize if you've
ever built a model in Keras.

00:39:57.820 --> 00:40:00.540
And then we have a couple
more examples here.

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:04.790
And then last one on Matplotlib.

00:40:04.790 --> 00:40:09.117
So that's a simple example
of how you can use SHAP.

00:40:09.117 --> 00:40:10.700
And if we could go
back to the slides.

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:17.520
YUFENG GUO: So during
the session today,

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:21.410
we've gone from getting
our dataset together,

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:25.370
loading it up with avocados, and
then pumping it through AutoML.

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:28.140
We did one with
"TensorFlow or not."

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:30.857
And we did a model which
categorized TensorFlow,

00:40:30.857 --> 00:40:33.440
Matplotlib, and pandas across a
couple of different libraries.

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:36.380
And so that, perhaps, is
like the more robust version

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:38.240
of that approach.

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:42.380
And then we went and built up
a similar model from scratch,

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:45.500
using a Bag of Words, or
bag of avocados approach.

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:48.740
And then we ran that in
Colab, deployed that model

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:54.380
to the Cloud AI Platform, and
used the prediction service

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:57.110
to actually make calls
and get predictions back.

00:40:57.110 --> 00:41:00.290
And then we fed that into
SHAP to interpret our model,

00:41:00.290 --> 00:41:03.290
to figure out what
words in each example

00:41:03.290 --> 00:41:07.310
contributed most and
contributed least to identifying

00:41:07.310 --> 00:41:10.663
the labels that it created.

00:41:10.663 --> 00:41:12.080
SARA ROBINSON: So
just to wrap up,

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:14.390
these are resources to all
of the different products

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:16.513
and open source projects
that we covered.

